WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.420
Follow the filtrate through the loop of Henle.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.380
Its two limbs run beside each other, but they

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.500
do not handle water and solute in the same

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:07.733
way.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:12.431
The supplied chapter describes
active sodium and chloride transport

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.188
from the thick ascending
limb into the medullary interstitium.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.435
This helps create a
concentrated environment around the loop.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.860
Water can then leave
the water-permeable descending limb, concentrating

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.430
the fluid that remains inside.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.050
In the ascending limb, solute leaves while water is

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.100
retained, so the tubular fluid becomes more dilute.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.385
Finally, follow the fluid toward the collecting duct.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.272
Antidiuretic hormone increases
water permeability there.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.020
More water returns to the body, and final urine

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:43.807
volume decreases.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:47.420
Keep the two ideas separate: the loop builds the

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:51.656
concentration gradient, while antidiuretic
hormone regulates the final water

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:52.170
adjustment.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.569
Motion, colors, and quantities are schematic.
